  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 144 PM CDT Fri May 29 2026\n\nPotential for Isolated Strong Storms on Sunday:\n\nThe slow-moving upper-level disturbance across the western CONUS\nwill finally pivot to the north-northeast early Sunday morning,\nwhich will lift a warm front through southeast Kansas and\nsouthwest Missouri. With a warm and moist airmass in place,\nisentropic ascent will likely trigger additional showers and \nthunderstorms. While convergence along this boundary looks \nunimpressive, a juiced thermo profile may allow for a few \nstronger storms capable of producing hail and strong winds. The\nStorm Prediction Center has outlined locations generally along\nand north of the I-44 corridor in a Marginal (level 1 of 5) risk\nfor severe thunderstorms. \n\nHot and Humid on Monday:\n\nEnsembles show general consensus in building an upper-level \nridge across the central CONUS early next week, with hot, humid \nair building underneath it. Air temperatures in the mid 80s to\nlow 90s with dew points near 70 degrees will make it feel quite\nswampy; heat indices are forecast to be in the 90s areawide. \nGlobal models suggest a weak ridge runner may result in \nadditional thunderstorm chances, mainly across the eastern \nOzarks.\n\nSeasonable Temperatures through Mid-week:\n\nEnsembles depict the aforementioned ridge axis shifting east\nthroughout the week, with several clusters suggesting some\nflattening of the upper-level pattern will occur. Indeed, NBM \npercentile data show temperatures moderating closer to more \nseasonable values in the low 80s Tuesday through Thursday.",
